The limit can also be found directly, without memorizing the three cases, as done here.

This calculation uses the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}g(x)
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{5x}{5x^4-4}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{5x}{x^{4}}}{\dfrac{5x^4-4}{x^{4}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide both sides by }x^{4}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{5\cancel{x}}{x^3\cdot\cancel{x}}}{\dfrac{5\cancel{x^4}}{\cancel{x^4}}-\dfrac{4}{x^4}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{5}{x^3}}{5-\dfrac{4}{x^4}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{0}{5-0}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{0}{5}
\\\\
&=0
\end{align}$
